    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">generative model is tasked at recovering the original input data from the diffused (noisy) data by learning to gradually reverse the diffusion process, step by step.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">In the latter steps of generation, diffusion models take charge of detail generation</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Our method accelerates diffusion models by using different-sized models to process different steps.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Diffusion models generate samples by reversing a xed forward diffusion process.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A diffusion model typically consists of two Markov chains of T steps. One of them is the forward process</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Diffusion models are a class of probabilistic generative models that learn to reverse a process that gradually degrades</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">generative model is tasked at recovering the original input data from the diffused (noisy) data by learning to gradually reverse the diffusion process</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Many methods based on diffusion models have been developed for text generation.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">diffusion models [ 20 , 50 ] have emerged as a leading solution for image generation [ 16 , 35 ], and for text-to-image synthesis</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Diffusion processes have been recently utilized to construct Diffusion Models , a class of generative models in deep learning</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Diffusion models have achieved great success in image synthesis through iterati</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">single denoising step can be regarded as a conventional deep learning model inference</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Diffusion models [ 25 , 62 , 67 ] are powerful deep generative models that emerge recently for high quality image generation</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">DDMs then learn to revert the forward process p ( x t 1 | x t ) with learnable parameters .</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">noise-based diffusion models, but they rely on score matching during the reverse process.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">a diffusion-based decoder that can generate an image conditioned on the image embedding.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Diffusion models (Sohl-Dickstein et al. 2015; Song and Ermon 2019) work by inverting a stepwise noise process using latent variables.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">increasing model size and decreasing patch size yields considerably improved diffusion models.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Diffusion models may be viewed as hierarchical variational autoencoders (VAEs) with two improvements: parameter sharing for the conditional distributions in the generative process</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A diffusion model typically consists of two Markov chains of T steps. One of them is the forward processalso known as the diffusion processwhich incrementally adds Gaussian noises</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Diffusion probabilistic models (DPMs) have been shown to generate high-quality images</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We feed this to the diffusion model via cross-attention</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Diffusion models [ 82 , 213 ] are a category of probabilistic generative models that learn to reverse a process that gradually degrades the training data structure</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">diffusion models are also notoriously expensive and data-hungry to train. They require large datasets and many iterations to capture the high-dimensional and complex data distributions.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">pre-trained diffusion model to be distilled</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">diffusion models can capture effectively complex conditional probability distributions and apply them to solve problems such as in-painting, super resolution or image-to-image translation</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">to train the diffusion model. The model training in practice is similar to reducing the variational upper bound on the negative log probability.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">fast sampling of diffusion models</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Diffusion models posit a forward diffusion process { z t }</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">diffusion models have emerged as a very powerful class of generative models, capable of generating high-</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Denoising diffusion models have emerged as a dominant approach for image generation, however they still suffer from slow convergence in training</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">diffusion models are also notoriously expensive and data-hungry to train.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">multiple steps (e.g., 25-1000) to generate a sample [11]. This is a potential hindrance in the practical applications of diffusion models, especially in the resource constrained environments.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Diffusion models have significantly advanced the fields of image, audio, and video generation, but they depend on an iterative sampling process that causes slow generation.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">diffusion models, especially in the resource constrained environments.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Training diffusion models is extremely resource intensive</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">discarding samples with small loss values in diffusion model training, thus mitigating the memorization.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">DiT-XL/2 outperforms all prior diffusion models</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">However, there is a class of models, known as diffusion models</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">diffusion in the latent space such that longer videos with more than one thousand frames can be produced.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Due to the nature of generative models, diffusion models are convenient for diverse denoising problems</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">analyze the general properties of diffusion models</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">diffusion models have been applied to a wide range of generative NLP tasks</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Neural diffusion models Finally, the reverse distribution is set to p</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">diffusion models. The essence of this method lies in learning an ordinary differential equation that follows a path from the source distribution to the target distribution.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">a diffusion model is trained to denoise the noised latent</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">a cascade of diffusion models for generating high-resolution images. These models are also conditioned on the text embeddings returned by the encoder.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">diffusion models can be understood as hierarchical variational autoencoders (VAEs) (Kingma &amp; Welling, 2013; Rezende et al., 2014; Snderby et al., 2016)</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We explore a new class of diffusion models based on the transformer architecture.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Conventional diffusion models learn the diffusion process flatly for each class</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the noise prediction task in diffusion models</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Diffusion models frequently employ U-Net models as denoisers</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">diffusion models have a stable training process and provide more diversity because they are likelihoodbased.</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
